        "title": "Ethology: Tinbergen's Four Questions",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Ethology is the biology of animal behavior — understanding behavior with the same rigor applied to anatomy or physiology. The field was formalized in the 1930s-1950s by Konrad Lorenz and Nikolaas Tinbergen (Nobel Prize 1973, shared with Karl von Frisch). Tinbergen's Four Questions remain the most powerful organizing framework for behavioral biology: they specify that any behavioral phenomenon requires four distinct, non-redundant levels of explanation."
